        <div id="testing-buying" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Testing, Amazon and Who Should Skip Jocko
          </h2>

          <p>
            Jocko Fuel says independent labs test every batch of its proteins for purity, potency and safety. It does not name the lab or a certification program on its product pages at the time of writing, and none of its proteins are presented as NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport. That is common for lifestyle brands, but it means drug-tested athletes should not use Jocko protein without first confirming a listing in one of those databases.
          </p>

          <p>
            For recreational buyers, the practical question is whether you trust the brand's own testing claims. If published certificates or a recognized seal matter to you, brands such as Momentous, Thorne and Transparent Labs offer more paperwork. Our <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">protein powder safety guide</a> explains what each certification covers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Jocko Fuel sells mainly from its own site, which is where subscription pricing and bundles live, and its Clear Protein Isolate comes with a 60-day money-back guarantee per the product page. If you buy through Amazon, choose a listing sold by Jocko Fuel or Amazon itself, check the best-by date on arrival, and avoid unusually cheap third-party sellers, which is good practice for any supplement.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Skip Jocko if</strong> you are a drug-tested athlete, if you want the most protein per dollar, if you are allergic to egg, which rules out Molk powder, or if allulose upsets your stomach. <strong>Buy it if</strong> you want dessert-style flavors without sucralose, like the idea of a whey and casein blend for between-meal shakes, or want a 30 g ready-to-drink shake with no added sugar.
          </p>

          <p>
            As always, if you have kidney disease, are pregnant, take medication or have food allergies, speak to your doctor or a registered dietitian before adding a protein supplement. Nothing on this page is medical advice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Judging protein by scoop size</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 34 g Molk scoop delivers 22 g of protein, while many 30 g whey scoops deliver 24 g. Compare grams of protein per serving and per dollar, not how big the scoop looks, or you will overestimate how much protein you are actually buying with each tub.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting the egg allergen</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Molk powder contains egg white protein as well as milk. Anyone with an egg allergy should avoid it and check every Jocko label, since the shakes and plant powder use different protein sources. If in doubt, ask your doctor or allergist before trying a new blend.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Stacking several allulose products</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Allulose is gentle in small amounts, but taking Molk, an energy drink and other allulose-sweetened products in one day can add up and cause bloating in sensitive people. Start with one serving a day and see how your stomach handles it before increasing your intake or adding a second shake.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming batch testing means sport certification</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Jocko says independent labs test every batch, but it names no NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport certification. Drug-tested athletes should buy only products listed in those databases, and should not rely on general testing claims from any brand, however well known its founder is.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying list price for single tubs</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Jocko's subscription and bundle pricing can cut costs noticeably, and the 13-serving plant tub is expensive per gram at list price. If you are a regular user, compare the subscription price per 25 g of protein against a mainstream whey before you commit to a recurring order.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

